  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "The problem",
      "title": "hash % N breaks when N changes",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Last lesson ended badly: with `shard = hash(key) % N`, going from 4 to 5 shards changes the answer for about 80% of keys. Nearly all the data moves.\n\nWe want a scheme where adding a server only moves the keys that server should take over — roughly 1/N of them.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "The trick is to stop tying placement to N at all."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "nodes": "4",
        "moved": "80%",
        "maxShare": "25%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "The ring",
      "title": "Bend the hash space into a ring",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Hash values run from 0 to 2³². Join the ends and you get a [[ring]].\n\nNow hash each **server’s** name too, and place it on the ring at that point. The servers glide to wherever their hashes put them — not evenly spaced, just wherever the hash lands.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Nothing here depends on how many servers there are. That’s the whole point."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Hash ring",
        "~ Servers placed at hash(server name)"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "nodes": "4"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Keys",
      "title": "Each key goes clockwise",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Hash each **key** onto the ring too. A key belongs to the **first server clockwise** from it. The coloured arcs show which server owns which stretch; each dot is a key, coloured by its owner.\n\nWatch a lookup travel clockwise from a key to its server.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "To find a key’s server: hash the key, walk clockwise, stop at the first server. Clients do this with a sorted list and a binary search."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Keys on the ring",
        "~ Each key owned by the next server clockwise"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "nodes": "4",
        "maxShare": "28%"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Add a server",
      "title": "Adding a server moves one arc",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Server E joins at its hash position, between C and B. It takes over only the keys in the arc just counter-clockwise of it — the ones that used to walk past it to C.\n\nEvery other key stays exactly where it was. The pulsing keys are the only ones that move.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Adding the 5th server moves about 1/5 of the keys — the minimum possible."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Server E",
        "~ 3 of 18 keys move (to E)",
        "~ Everything else stays put"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "nodes": "5",
        "moved": "17%",
        "maxShare": "28%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "ring-key-322": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-336": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-350": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "k1": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Remove one",
      "title": "Removing a server moves one arc too",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Back to four servers — and now C dies. Its keys simply continue clockwise to the next server, D. Nobody else is affected.\n\nCompare that with `% N`, where losing one server would reshuffle almost everything.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Amazon’s Dynamo, Cassandra and many caches and CDNs place data this way. Close cousins — rendezvous and jump hashing — get the same result without a ring."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Server C",
        "~ C’s 4 keys move to D"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "nodes": "3",
        "moved": "22%",
        "maxShare": "44%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "c-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-296": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-322": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-322-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-336": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-336-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-350": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-350-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "e": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "k1": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Uneven",
      "title": "Random positions, uneven arcs",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Servers land wherever their hash puts them, so arcs are uneven. With C gone, D now owns a huge stretch of the ring — and gets far more keys and traffic than A or B.\n\nWith only a few servers, luck alone can leave one doing twice the work of another.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Uneven arcs mean uneven load — exactly the hot spot sharding was meant to avoid."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ D owns 44% of the keys"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "nodes": "3",
        "maxShare": "44%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "c-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "d-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-296": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-322": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-322-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-336": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-336-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-350": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-350-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "e": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "k1": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Virtual nodes",
      "title": "Virtual nodes even it out",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Give each server several positions on the ring — [[virtual nodes]] (the diamonds) — by hashing `A#1`, `A#2`, … Each server now owns many small arcs instead of one big one.\n\nShares even out, and when a server leaves, its keys spread across *all* the others instead of dumping on one neighbour.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Real systems use anywhere from 16 to a few hundred virtual nodes per server. Stronger servers can get more of them."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ 3 positions per server",
        "~ Biggest share 44% → 33%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "nodes": "3",
        "maxShare": "33%"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "c-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "d-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ring-key-82": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-296": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-322": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-322-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-336": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-336-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-350": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ring-key-350-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "e": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "k1": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Replicas",
      "title": "Copies on the next servers clockwise",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "One copy per key isn’t enough — a dead server would lose its data. So each key is stored on the first **three** distinct servers clockwise: its owner plus the next two.\n\nFollow the key at 160°: copy 1 on A, copy 2 on B, copy 3 on C. If A dies, B and C still have it, and the next server along takes over A’s share.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Dynamo calls this the preference list. With virtual nodes, skip positions owned by a server you already picked."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Each key stored on 3 servers",
        "~ Survives any single failure"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "nodes": "4",
        "maxShare": "28%"
      },
      "overrides": {
